[Event "Paris"]
[Site "Paris FRA"]
[Date "1750.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Legall de Kermeur"]
[Black "Saint Brie"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C41"]
[Opening "Philidor Defence"]
[CMIntro "The oldest trick in the book: White gives up the queen and mates with the minor pieces. Learn Legal's mate!"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 d6 3. Bc4 Bg4 4. Nc3 g6 {This slow move is a mistake.} 5. Nxe5!
{White leaves the queen to be taken!} 5... Bxd1 6. Bxf7+ Ke7 7. Nd5#
{Checkmate with a bishop and two knights. This pattern is called "Legal's mate".}
1-0

[Event "London"]
[Site "London ENG"]
[Date "1851.06.21"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Adolf Anderssen"]
[Black "Lionel Kieseritzky"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C33"]
[Opening "King's Gambit Accepted"]
[CMIntro "The Immortal Game - the most famous example of Romantic chess: White sacrifices a bishop, both rooks and the queen, and still checkmates."]

1. e4 e5 2. f4 {The King's Gambit: White offers a pawn for quick development.}
2... exf4 3. Bc4 Qh4+ 4. Kf1 b5 5. Bxb5 Nf6 6. Nf3 Qh6 7. d3 Nh5 8. Nh4 Qg5
9. Nf5 c6 10. g4 Nf6 11. Rg1 cxb5 12. h4 Qg6 13. h5 Qg5 14. Qf3 Ng8 15. Bxf4 Qf6
16. Nc3 Bc5 17. Nd5 Qxb2 18. Bd6 {White does not care about the rooks: Black's
king is in danger!} 18... Bxg1 19. e5 Qxa1+ 20. Ke2 Na6 21. Nxg7+ Kd8 22. Qf6+
{The final sacrifice: the queen!} 22... Nxf6 23. Be7#
{Checkmate with just three minor pieces.} 1-0

[Event "Berlin"]
[Site "Berlin GER"]
[Date "1852.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Adolf Anderssen"]
[Black "Jean Dufresne"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C52"]
[Opening "Evans Gambit"]
[CMIntro "The Evergreen Game: a sparkling attack by the same Adolf Anderssen, ending with a beautiful queen sacrifice."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. b4 {The Evans Gambit: a pawn for fast development.}
4... Bxb4 5. c3 Ba5 6. d4 exd4 7. O-O d3 8. Qb3 Qf6 9. e5 Qg6 10. Re1 Nge7
11. Ba3 b5 12. Qxb5 Rb8 13. Qa4 Bb6 14. Nbd2 Bb7 15. Ne4 Qf5 16. Bxd3 Qh5
17. Nf6+ gxf6 18. exf6 Rg8 19. Rad1 Qxf3 20. Rxe7+ Nxe7 21. Qxd7+ {A queen
sacrifice to open the way for the bishops.} 21... Kxd7 22. Bf5+ Ke8 23. Bd7+ Kf8
24. Bxe7# 1-0

[Event "Paris"]
[Site "Paris FRA"]
[Date "1858.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Paul Morphy"]
[Black "Duke Karl / Count Isouard"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C41"]
[Opening "Philidor Defence"]
[CMIntro "The Opera Game, played during an opera in Paris. Morphy shows how powerful fast development is."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 Bg4 4. dxe5 Bxf3 5. Qxf3 dxe5 6. Bc4 {White threatens
Qxf7 checkmate.} 6... Nf6 7. Qb3 Qe7 8. Nc3 c6 9. Bg5 b5 10. Nxb5 cxb5
11. Bxb5+ Nbd7 12. O-O-O {Every white piece is in play, while Black's bishop
and rook on the king's side have not moved.} 12... Rd8 13. Rxd7 Rxd7 14. Rd1 Qe6
15. Bxd7+ Nxd7 16. Qb8+ Nxb8 17. Rd8# {A queen sacrifice and a rook mate.} 1-0

[Event "Amsterdam"]
[Site "Amsterdam NED"]
[Date "1889.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Emanuel Lasker"]
[Black "Johann Bauer"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "A03"]
[Opening "Bird's Opening"]
[CMIntro "The future World Champion Emanuel Lasker sacrifices both bishops to tear open the black king's shelter - a famous attacking pattern."]

1. f4 d5 2. e3 Nf6 3. b3 e6 4. Bb2 Be7 5. Bd3 b6 6. Nc3 Bb7 7. Nf3 Nbd7 8. O-O
O-O 9. Ne2 c5 10. Ng3 Qc7 11. Ne5 Nxe5 12. Bxe5 Qc6 13. Qe2 a6 14. Nh5 Nxh5
15. Bxh7+ {The first bishop sacrifice.} 15... Kxh7 16. Qxh5+ Kg8 17. Bxg7
{And the second one!} 17... Kxg7 18. Qg4+ Kh7 19. Rf3 e5 20. Rh3+ Qh6 21. Rxh6+
Kxh6 22. Qd7 {This double attack wins one of the bishops back.} 22... Bf6
23. Qxb7 Kg7 24. Rf1 Rab8 25. Qd7 Rfd8 26. Qg4+ Kf8 27. fxe5 Bg7 28. e6 Rb7
29. Qg6 f6 30. Rxf6+ Bxf6 31. Qxf6+ Ke8 32. Qh8+ Ke7 33. Qg7+ Kxe6 34. Qxb7 Rd6
35. Qxa6 d4 36. exd4 cxd4 37. h4 d3 38. Qxd3 1-0

[Event "Hastings"]
[Site "Hastings ENG"]
[Date "1895.08.17"]
[Round "10"]
[White "Wilhelm Steinitz"]
[Black "Curt von Bardeleben"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C54"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Giuoco Piano"]
[CMIntro "The first World Champion, Wilhelm Steinitz, keeps the black king in the centre and finishes with a rook that refuses to be captured."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d4 exd4 6. cxd4 Bb4+ 7. Nc3 d5
8. exd5 Nxd5 9. O-O Be6 10. Bg5 Be7 11. Bxd5 Bxd5 12. Nxd5 Qxd5 13. Bxe7 Nxe7
14. Re1 f6 15. Qe2 Qd7 16. Rac1 c6 17. d5 {A pawn sacrifice to open lines
against the uncastled king.} 17... cxd5 18. Nd4 Kf7 19. Ne6 Rhc8 20. Qg4 g6
21. Ng5+ Ke8 22. Rxe7+ {The rook cannot be taken: every capture loses.} 22...
Kf8 23. Rf7+ Kg8 24. Rg7+ Kh8 25. Rxh7+ {Black left the tournament hall instead
of resigning. Steinitz then showed a forced mate in ten moves.} 1-0

